{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 120,
    "dDurationMs": 2680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey Everyone. Just letting you \nknow there’s new limited edition  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2800,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Merch at the Merch store. More \ninfo at the end of the episode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 6520,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So you’ve decided to jump into a black hole. Good \nnews: as long as the black hole is big enough you  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 12200,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can sail through the event horizon without harm \nand get to experience the interior of the black  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 17240,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hole before you’re annihilated by the central \nsingularity. Or so we once thought. These days,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 23520,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quite a few physicists believe that the \nonly way to avoid horrible contradictions  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 28560,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in fundamental physics generated by black \nholes is for all them to be surrounded by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 33760,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "screens of extreme energy that prevent \nanything from ever entering the event  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39040,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "horizon. Sounds outlandish? Welcome \nto black holes. So let’s find out why  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44560,
    "dDurationMs": 12120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "many of our most brilliant physicists take \nthese black hole firewalls deadly seriously."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 56680,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Einstein’s general theory of relativity has been \nwildly successful at describing the behaviour  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60520,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of space and time and gravity and its crazy \npredictions have been verified over and over,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65440,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from time dilation to black holes to gravitational \nwaves. And it all started with this one idea—what  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 70640,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Einstein called his happiest thought—that there \nis a fundamental equivalence between the feeling  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 76840,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of floating you have when falling and the \nsame feeling of floating in the absence of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 80720,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravitational fields. All of general relativity \ncame from following this equivalence principle to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85080,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its logical conclusions, plus the thing about the \nspeed of light being constant for everyone. That’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 90320,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "why it makes physicists very sceptical when a new \nidea appears to violate this principle. But what  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98400,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if the alternative to violating equivalence is \nviolating something that’s even more fundamental?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 105120,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In recent episodes, we saw how the prospect of \nblack holes either destroying or duplicating  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 110240,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum information violates something called \nunitarity which is as foundational to quantum  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 116480,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mechanics as the equivalence principle \nis to general relativity. TLDW: if black  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 121560,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "holes both swallow quantum information and also \nradiate that information as Hawking radiation,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 126480,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then the same bit of quantum info is in two \nplaces at once and physics breaks. But if  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 132120,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you don’t let quantum information fall \ninto the black hole in the first place  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135880,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then you violate the equivalence principle, \nbecause this principle tells us that nothing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 140400,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "special or weird should happen to anything \nfalling through an event horizon—as long  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 144480,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as that horizon is large enough so \nthere’s no observable tidal force."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 149440,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This catch-22—violate unitarity \nor equivalence—is the black hole  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 154200,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "information paradox. One way around this \nis to argue that qubit duplication is fine  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 160680,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as long as there’s no observer—no conceivable \nreference frame—that can access both versions  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 165720,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the information. Now this is black hole \ncomplementarity, and in our previous episode  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 171000,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the topic we followed the adventures of \nAlice and Bob as they attempted to outsmart  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 175560,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reality and observe both the swallowed and \nthe Hawking radiated qubit. But reality was  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 181840,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "too smart for them—it seemed impossible for \none observer to ever see duplicate qubits."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 186480,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For a while, this black hole complementarity was \nthought to have solved the black hole information  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 190440,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "paradox, but it turns out it’s not as neat a \nsolution as we had hoped. Not long after these  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 196200,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "early Alice and Bob expeditions were conceived, \na new thought experiment appeared that may have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 201920,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "found a way to outsmart reality after all. \nThe thought experiment didn’t just reveal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 206760,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a way to detect quantum cloning, but also \nnecessitated the appearance of a firewall—a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 212720,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "screen of extreme energy that prevents anyone \never entering a black hole in the first place."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 217960,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This final proposal saves unitarity, but violates \nthe equivalence principle so utterly that many  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 225000,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "physicists despise the idea. And yet some \nargue that the chain of logic behind the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 231200,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "firewall is so robust that we need to seriously \nconsider throwing equivalence out the window."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 237320,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So how did we end up in this unfortunate \nstate of contradictory affairs? Well,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242240,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to understand that we need to go back to the \nblack hole information paradox and understand it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 247080,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in terms of not just quantum information, but also \nquantum entanglement. Quantum entanglement is when  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 253600,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a pair of quantum objects are correlated \nwith each other in a sort of fuzzy way,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 258360,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "such that the information about their individual \nstates is undetermined—it’s in a superposition of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 263600,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "multiple states at once. But this superposition of \nstates is correlated between the entangled pair,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 269280,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "such that the measurement of one defines the \nproperties of the other. We have episodes on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 274040,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this crazy stuff if you want to dig into that \nweird hole. But for now just know that quantum  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 278760,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "entanglement is ubiquitous and the information \nabout quantum systems is largely woven into webs  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 285280,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of entanglement. That’s why entanglement \nis important for the information paradox."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 291680,
    "dDurationMs": 7400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So let’s look at entanglement in black holes. As \na black hole grows, so does its entropy. This is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 299080,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the finding by Steven Hawking and Jacob Bekenstein \nthat led to the black hole information paradox in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 303960,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the first place. Entropy is basically a measure \nof the information that’s hidden in a system  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 308360,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that we can’t access. Black hole entropy grows \nbecause almost all the details about the stuff  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 313720,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that fell in is lost to us. Most physicists think \nthat this information has to be somehow encoded  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 320680,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the horizon and then must escape as Hawking \nradiation, because if it didn’t then it would  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 327760,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "blink out of existence when that same Hawking \nradiation causes the black hole to evaporate."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 332560,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The high entropy at the horizon means that \nthe degrees of freedom on that horizon are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 338760,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "highly entangled with each other—the quantum \ninformation about their original pre-falling-in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 344400,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "state is scrambled across the horizon. \nNow when the first particle of Hawking  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 349920,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "radiation leaks out it contains almost no \ninformation about the stuff that fell in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 354040,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because that information is spread across its \nentanglements with other horizon particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 359840,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But as more and more particles leave, we can \nstart to see patterns. Information about the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 365120,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "material that orginally fell in could, in \nprinciple, be slowly reconstructed from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 369880,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Hawking radiation. That really only \nbecomes possible when the particles of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 374600,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hawking radiation are no longer exclusively \nentangled with the inaccessible horizon,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 380680,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but rather with their entangled\nother particles of Hawking radiation.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 385200,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s a very specific transition point when \nthe qubits remaining on the horizon are mostly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 390400,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "entangled with qubits in the expanding cloud of \nHawking radiation—we would say that at this moment"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 397160,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the entanglement between the black hole \nand the Hawking cloud is at its maximum,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 402200,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and it happens when around half the \nmass of the black hole is evaporated,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 406760,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "although in terms of time it’s about 90% of the \nway to complete evaporation. This moment is called  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 413760,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Page time, after Don Page who figured out how \nthe entanglement entropy of black hole horizons  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 420000,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "must first rise as information and \nthen shrink. After this time it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 425400,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "becomes increasingly possible to find and \ndecode pairs of entangled Hawking qubits."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 432000,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So let’s consider a single qubit that’s \nstill encoded on the horizon at around  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 437080,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Page time. It’s entangled with a \ndistant particle of Hawking radiation.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 442200,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It now wants to join its quantum sibling \nand become Hawking radiation itself."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 447760,
    "dDurationMs": 3400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So a popular way to describe \nHawking radiation is like this:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 451160,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the vacuum of space, pairs of matter and \nantimatter particles are constantly appearing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 455440,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and vanishing. If this happens at an event \nhorizon, one of these particles can be captured,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 461600,
    "dDurationMs": 7160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "leaving the other free to escape and effectively \nbecome a real particle of Hawking radiation. A  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 468760,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more precise way to describe this is in terms \nof quantum field theory—and we obviously have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 473720,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an episode on this. We can describe the vacuum of \nspace as consisting of equal parts positive and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 479600,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "negative frequency modes of the quantum fields in \nthat space—equal and opposite vibrations in those  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 485520,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fields that cancel each other out perfectly. \nBlack hole horizons perturb these modes,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 491520,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the non-perfect cancelling leads to a \nnon-perfect vacuum. The quantum fields end up in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 496520,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "excited states, and this manifests as particles—of \nHawking radiation. However you like to describe  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 502480,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hawking radiation, we have one thing being \nswallowed by the black hole and one escaping."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 508840,
    "dDurationMs": 7880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And listen, because this is very important—these \npairs of swallowed and radiated things have to be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 516720,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "entangled with each other. In fact it’s been \nargued that the fabric of space is stitched  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 521520,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "together by the entanglement between these \nvirtual particles or modes. The entanglement  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 527040,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of adjacent fluctuations is fundamental. In fact, \nit seems that without this entanglement there  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 532840,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is no space. That’s something we’ll come back \nto another time, but keep it in mind for now."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 537840,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so back to our qubit sitting at the horizon \nwaiting to radiate away. It’s entangled with a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 544160,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "distant qubit of Hawking radiation, but \nit also needs to be entangled with an  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 548760,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "adjacent qubit just below the horizon. \nWithout the latter there is no partner  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 553640,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particle or mode that it can sacrifice to \nthe black hole in order to escape. So this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 560000,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "qubit must be entangled twice—with both the \nHawking cloud and the black hole interior."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 565720,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You might say it’s quite promiscuous \nin its entanglement. Naughty qubit  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 571400,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and quantum mechanics does have \nrules about these things. In fact,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 575840,
    "dDurationMs": 8880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we know that entanglement has to be monogamous. \nMonogamy of entanglement says that if a pair of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 584720,
    "dDurationMs": 7240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "qubits are maximally entangled with each other \nthen neither qubit can share any entanglement  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 591960,
    "dDurationMs": 7480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with a third qubit outside this pair. Monogamy of \nentanglement is closely related to the no-cloning  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 599440,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "theorem, and absolutely violates \nunitarity to violate this monogomy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 605760,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If we want to avoid over-entangling our black \nhole it seems we need to break one of these  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 611080,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "entanglements. Because the entanglement over the \nHawking radiation holds the information we want  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 616920,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to preserve, it’s probably safer to break the \nentanglement between qubits above and below the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 621560,
    "dDurationMs": 7720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "horizon. Unfortunately there are dire consequences \nto doing that. It takes energy to break any  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 629280,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "entanglement. You can calculate the amount of \nenergy required to break the links between the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 634360,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "swallowed and radiated particles at the horizon. \nAnd it is a lot of energy. In fact in order  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 641400,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for all of the Hawking radiation to break its \nentanglement connection with the black hole there  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 647520,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would need to be a screen of energy just above the \nhorizon so intense that nothing could ever cross  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 653760,
    "dDurationMs": 8400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that horizon without being completely thermalized. \nSevering entanglement requires a firewall."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 662160,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Incidentally, the firewall solves the \nviolations of unitarity in two ways:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 668200,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it breaks the entanglement between the Hawking \nradiation and the interior of the black hole,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 672200,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so monogamy of entanglement is saved. And it also \nstops anyone observing the interior of the black  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 678200,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hole by turning them to plasma. That makes it \nextra impossible to even think about observing the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 684080,
    "dDurationMs": 7240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "same qubit both inside and outside the black hole. \nAnd this firewall wouldn’t be visible to a distant  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 691320,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "observer—the extreme gravitational redshift \nof the radiation emerging from a miniscule  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 697800,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Planck length above the horizon would turn that \nradiation into very faint Hawking radiation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 705720,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so firewalls sound cool and \nuseful even if they ruin our trip  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 709560,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into the black hole. Unfortunately, the \nfirewall hypothesis pretty thoroughly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 715000,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "violates the sacred idea we started \nwith: the equivalence principle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 719520,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "More formally, the equivalence principle \nstates that there’s no experiment you can  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 724680,
    "dDurationMs": 9080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "do on the local patch of spacetime to distinguish \nbetween this freefall or gravity-free floating,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 733760,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or between standing still against a \ngravitational field with a certain  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 738120,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "g-force versus acceleration absent \ngravity with the same g-force."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 743360,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, if Alice is in freefall towards an event \nhorizon, the equivalence principle says that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 749360,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "she should measure a quantum vacuum consistent \nwith empty space and should see no local sign  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 755160,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "revealing the location of the event horizon. But \nthe firewall is a hell of a sign. It reveals to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 761480,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Alice that the vacuum is massively altered \nby some apparently non-local influence."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 767160,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The black hole firewall paradox is a refinement \nof the black hole information paradox. It’s also  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 773440,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "called the AMPS paradox after the physicists \nwho first outlined it in 2012: Ahmed Almheiri,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 780200,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Donald Marolf, Joseph Polchinski, and James Sully. \nWhether or not firewalls exist—and physicists are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 788120,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "still divided on this—the main point of the \nAMPS paper was to show that the black hole  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 794040,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "information paradox is still a paradox. In \nparticular, it’s not fully solved by black  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 800320,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hole complementarity. As we saw in our episode \non the topic, it’s possible to hedge around the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 806840,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "problem of quantum cloning by noting that no \npossible observation can detect that cloning."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 812600,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the firewall paradox grants a way to \ncheat. For example, Alice could descend  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 817640,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "very close to the event horizon to see if \nthere’s a firewall. If there is then the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 822240,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "equivalence principle is violated. That’s bad. \nIf there isn’t, she has evidence that quantum  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 828160,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "entanglement must exist across the event \nhorizon. She could then back out and see  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 833320,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that quantum entanglement also exists between \nthe horizon and the past Hawking radiation,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 837520,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "revealing that monogamy of entanglement is \nviolated and with it unitarity. Also bad."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 844520,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So the power of the firewall argument \nis that Alice can learn whether the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 848720,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exterior and interior of the black hole \nare entangled without having to enter  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 852760,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the black hole. That seems to defeat the \nmain value of black hole complementarity,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 857560,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even if this complementarity seems \nto be still needed for other things."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 862840,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If firewalls do exist, then it’s not just \nthat the black hole interior is inaccessible,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 867880,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it means the black hole interior may not \nexist. Breaking the entanglement between  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 874680,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the vacuum modes across the horizon is the \nsame thing as breaking the stitching of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 879800,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fabric of space itself. In a regular \nblack hole, that spatial fabric ends  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 884240,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at the central singularity. If firewalls \nexist, then it’s like the singularity has  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 889720,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expanded to the event horizon, and that there’s \nliterally nothing—not even a vacuum—beneath it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 897400,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, what’s it all mean? Well this is not a \nsolved problem. Some, including the AMPS four,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 903720,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "argue that breaking the equivalence principle \nis less radical than breaking unitarity,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 908800,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and so favour the firewall. But breaking \nequivalence is radical enough, and many  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 913680,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would prefer a solution which doesn’t violate \neither. For example we have Leonard Susskind  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 918800,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and Raphael Bousso, who both point to other \nvery general options. If we don’t want to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 923920,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "break unitarity or the equivalence principle, \nwe have to admit that the theories we use  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 928320,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to describe the happenings above the event \nhorizon are themselves broken. For example,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 933320,
    "dDurationMs": 3160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that the quantum field theory we’re \nusing to describe these vacuum modes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 936480,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and Hawking radiation break down. But this \nis the same QFT that works perfectly well  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 942040,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in situations that are really no more extreme \nthan the event horizon of a large black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 946800,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are some other pretty funky notions. For \nexample Gary Horowitz and Juan Maldecena have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 952560,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a scheme to reframe quantum field theory with \nboth forward and backward causal directions,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 957720,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which enables the information swallowed by the \nblack hole to effectively teleport back out to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 962200,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the horizon qubit. This avoids the need to break \nentanglement and so ignite firewalls. Or maybe a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 968280,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "full theory of quantum gravity will help us \nto avoid information-swallowing black holes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 972600,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "after all. For example, fuzzballs of string \ntheory propose that matter unravels into giant  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 978520,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "balls of elementary strings below the event \nhorizon. These happen to offer a way to move  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 983200,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum information from below the horizon back \ninto the universe, again avoiding the firewall."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 989840,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Long story short is that the black hole firewall \nparadox tells us that our current ways of thinking  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 996120,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about black holes need to be cleaned up. \nMany believe that this little glitch in the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1000760,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "consistency of our best theories of nature is the \nmost promising path to a deeper theory that could  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1005560,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "unite quantum mechanics and general relativity. \nThese ideas may even lead us to an idea we’ll  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1012400,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "explore soon—which is to an understanding of \nhow space emerges from quantum entanglement,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1018040,
    "dDurationMs": 7660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and how, if we unstitch that entanglement, \nwe get firewalls at the end of space time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1025700,
    "dDurationMs": 5980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Twelve years ago on July 4th, 2012 the ATLAS \nand CMS collaborations announced the discovery  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1031680,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of a new particle to a packed auditorium \nat CERN. It was revealed to be the Higgs  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1036360,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Boson - the particle that makes the existence \nof matter and interactions as we know them  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1041240,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "possible. To celebrate the fireworks of that \ncollision we’ve designed our very own limited  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1046480,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "edition UV glow print Higgs T-Shirt \u0026 Hoodie. \nBoth the t-shirt and hoodie are UV charged,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1052920,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so after being out in the sun they’ll begin \nto glow. Because of the UV printing process,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1057520,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we only are able to print a limited number. If \nyou’d like to receive your shirt before July 4th,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1064520,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then you’ll need to put your order in by \nJune 23rd. Check out pbsspacetime.com/shop  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1071160,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to check them out. There’s a \nlink the in the description."
    } ]
  } ]
}
